<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is DaRon Holmes II on a Rookie Contract?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Holmes is paid <strong>$3.37m</strong> because he went 22nd in 2024, and the rookie scale sets what that slot earns whether a player appears or not.</p>
<ul>
<li>Draft position fixed all four seasons before he had played a professional minute.</li>
<li>A rookie scale contract keeps paying through injury, which is why he was paid in full for a season he missed entirely.</li>
<li>He is a 6’9″ big man who blocks shots and can step out to shoot, which is the reason he went in the first round.</li>
<li>An Achilles injury at 21 is the kind that changes what a player is, and nobody yet knows what he is after it.</li>
<li>Denver has one more option, due by November 2, 2026, and <strong>$5.55m</strong> of the contract is unguaranteed.</li>
</ul>
<p>He has been paid <strong>$9.66m</strong> so far for parts of two seasons of basketball.</p>

</div><div class="bde-shortcode-506-159 bde-shortcode"><div class="nba-fin" data-nba-fin="why">
